WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.380
How can a cell answer a signal in about a millisecond?

2
00:00:04.480 --> 00:00:08.360
Most receptors hand their message to a G protein or a kinase.

3
00:00:09.520 --> 00:00:15.100
An ion channel skips that chain: the signal opens a hole, ions rush through, and

4
00:00:15.100 --> 00:00:16.220
voltage shifts at once.

5
00:00:19.700 --> 00:00:23.020
Two kinds of protein move ions across a membrane.

6
00:00:24.280 --> 00:00:30.100
A pump binds ions, uses ATP, and pushes them uphill, only a few hundred each second.

7
00:00:31.300 --> 00:00:32.840
A channel is a gated pore.

8
00:00:34.120 --> 00:00:36.880
Open it, and millions pour downhill every second.

9
00:00:38.260 --> 00:00:41.160
Pumps build gradients slowly; channels spend them fast.

10
00:00:42.250 --> 00:00:45.210
What makes an ion run downhill through an open pore?

11
00:00:46.490 --> 00:00:51.530
Two forces. Crowded ions spread from high concentration toward low.

12
00:00:53.010 --> 00:00:58.450
Membrane voltage pulls on their charge; a resting neuron sits about minus −70 mV

13
00:00:58.450 --> 00:01:02.950
inside. Together, they form the electrochemical gradient.

14
00:01:04.050 --> 00:01:08.670
For each ion, one membrane voltage balances those two forces exactly.

15
00:01:10.050 --> 00:01:12.890
That balance point is its equilibrium potential.

16
00:01:14.630 --> 00:01:20.610
Na+ is crowded outside, so only a strongly positive inside, about plus +60 mV, holds

17
00:01:20.610 --> 00:01:27.670
it back. K+ is crowded inside, so its balance point, about minus −90 sits below

18
00:01:27.670 --> 00:01:28.670
rest.

19
00:01:29.083 --> 00:01:30.503
Here is the core rule.

20
00:01:31.703 --> 00:01:36.503
An open channel pulls membrane voltage toward its ion’s equilibrium potential.

21
00:01:37.763 --> 00:01:43.003
The pull is the driving force: membrane voltage minus equilibrium potential.

22
00:01:44.283 --> 00:01:50.383
For Na+, −70 − (+60) = −130 mV, a huge gap.

23
00:01:51.603 --> 00:01:56.483
For K+, −70 − (−90) = just +20 mV.

24
00:01:56.917 --> 00:02:02.557
Picture a resting neuron with two closed channels: one for Na+, one for K+.

25
00:02:04.157 --> 00:02:05.157
Pause and predict.

26
00:02:06.157 --> 00:02:09.177
If we open each in turn, which ion rushes harder?

27
00:02:10.457 --> 00:02:12.557
And which way does voltage move each time?

28
00:02:18.467 --> 00:02:22.707
Na+’s gap is huge, so it pours inward and voltage shoots upward.

29
00:02:24.087 --> 00:02:29.107
Each ion carries positive charge in, making the inside less negative; that is

30
00:02:29.107 --> 00:02:33.427
depolarisation. Now shut Na+ and open K+.

31
00:02:34.867 --> 00:02:37.707
Its gap is small, so K+ leaks out gently.

32
00:02:39.167 --> 00:02:42.527
Losing positive charge nudges the voltage slightly downward.

33
00:02:43.650 --> 00:02:48.990
A glass pipette sealed onto a membrane patch, the patch clamp, records one channel

34
00:02:48.990 --> 00:02:53.810
at a time. The current jumps between shut and open at a fixed height.

35
00:02:55.090 --> 00:02:57.510
A stronger signal does not widen the pore.

36
00:02:58.850 --> 00:03:02.590
Instead, it raises the share of time the channel spends open.

37
00:03:03.650 --> 00:03:09.530
Few ions need to cross, because a thin membrane turns a tiny charge imbalance into a

38
00:03:09.530 --> 00:03:15.090
large voltage. Bulk concentrations barely shift, and nothing new is made.

39
00:03:16.310 --> 00:03:21.990
A channel passing several ions pulls voltage between their equilibrium potentials.

40
00:03:21.990 --> 00:03:26.030
Spend the stored gradient, and voltage becomes a fast signal.
